1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,140
It's ten at night.

2
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:02,340
You're hungry.

3
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,680
You tap one button, and thirty minutes later,

4
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,340
a biryani shows up at your door.

5
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:07,620
Like magic.

6
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,560
Except, it's not magic.

7
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:12,260
It's a restaurant kitchen, a bunch of prediction

8
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,820
models, and one rider on a scooter, racing

9
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:15,760
a traffic signal.

10
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,580
In India, apps like Swiggy and Zomato deliver

11
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,840
millions of food orders every single day.

12
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:23,100
So tonight, we follow one order.

13
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:24,540
One biryani.

14
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:27,200
Five stops on the order tracker, from your

15
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:28,020
tap to your door.

16
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:30,340
Oh, and at some point, the app will tell

17
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,360
you: your rider is two minutes away.

18
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:33,900
Start a timer.

19
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:34,980
Order placed.

20
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:39,207
The moment you pay, your order flies to

21
00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:40,787
a tablet in the restaurant kitchen.

22
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:42,167
It beeps.

23
00:00:42,227 --> 00:00:43,027
Loudly.

24
00:00:43,227 --> 00:00:44,607
Until someone taps accept.

25
00:00:45,307 --> 00:00:47,667
Now the app has to make a guess: how long

26
00:00:47,687 --> 00:00:48,827
will this biryani take?

27
00:00:49,327 --> 00:00:51,507
That guess is called food preparation time,

28
00:00:51,527 --> 00:00:53,587
and a machine learning model makes it.

29
00:00:53,867 --> 00:00:56,147
It looks at what you ordered, how many items,

30
00:00:56,307 --> 00:00:58,367
and how busy that kitchen is right now.

31
00:00:58,927 --> 00:01:00,927
One biryani on a Tuesday afternoon?

32
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:01,687
Quick.

33
00:01:01,707 --> 00:01:03,767
Forty orders on a Friday night?

34
00:01:03,787 --> 00:01:04,667
Good luck.

35
00:01:05,107 --> 00:01:06,987
And this guess matters more than anything.

36
00:01:07,477 --> 00:01:09,607
Because it decides exactly when to send

37
00:01:09,627 --> 00:01:10,427
your rider.

38
00:01:13,167 --> 00:01:14,167
Here's the clever bit.

39
00:01:14,727 --> 00:01:16,467
The app does not grab the nearest rider

40
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:17,467
the second you order.

41
00:01:17,967 --> 00:01:20,007
Too early, and the rider stands around the

42
00:01:20,027 --> 00:01:20,907
kitchen, waiting.

43
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:23,967
Too late, and your biryani sits on the counter,

44
00:01:23,987 --> 00:01:24,927
going cold.

45
00:01:25,407 --> 00:01:26,567
So the app times it.

46
00:01:27,027 --> 00:01:29,327
It's called just in time assignment: the

47
00:01:29,347 --> 00:01:31,347
rider should walk in right as the food is

48
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:32,167
packed.

49
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:34,367
Think of it as two races running at once.

50
00:01:35,087 --> 00:01:36,507
Race one, the kitchen cooking.

51
00:01:37,267 --> 00:01:39,567
Race two, the rider riding to the restaurant.

52
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:42,507
Your wait is whichever race is slower, plus

53
00:01:42,527 --> 00:01:43,447
the ride to your door.

54
00:01:43,947 --> 00:01:45,807
And if two orders are going the same way?

55
00:01:46,247 --> 00:01:47,347
One rider carries both.

56
00:01:47,787 --> 00:01:48,927
That's called batching.

57
00:01:52,033 --> 00:01:52,833
Food packed.

58
00:01:53,073 --> 00:01:53,873
Bag zipped.

59
00:01:54,093 --> 00:01:54,893
Picked up!

60
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:56,953
Now that tiny scooter on your screen starts

61
00:01:56,973 --> 00:01:58,733
moving, and that part is real.

62
00:01:59,413 --> 00:02:01,633
The rider's phone keeps sending its GPS

63
00:02:01,653 --> 00:02:04,093
location, and the app predicts the arrival

64
00:02:04,113 --> 00:02:06,753
time using the roads, live traffic, and

65
00:02:06,773 --> 00:02:07,993
thousands of past trips.

66
00:02:08,673 --> 00:02:10,653
Then comes the last mile, where the plan

67
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:11,513
meets reality.

68
00:02:11,873 --> 00:02:12,733
A red signal.

69
00:02:13,213 --> 00:02:15,053
A cow, having a meeting in the middle of

70
00:02:15,073 --> 00:02:15,813
the road.

71
00:02:15,833 --> 00:02:17,513
And the hardest part of the whole trip:

72
00:02:17,773 --> 00:02:19,253
the last hundred metres.

73
00:02:19,273 --> 00:02:20,353
The security gate.

74
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:23,093
And an address that says: Tower B, behind

75
00:02:23,113 --> 00:02:24,533
the temple, near the big tree.

76
00:02:25,493 --> 00:02:27,253
Meanwhile, on your screen: your rider is

77
00:02:27,273 --> 00:02:28,073
two minutes away.

78
00:02:28,873 --> 00:02:29,513
Still.

79
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:30,633
It has been nine minutes.

80
00:02:33,733 --> 00:02:34,533
Ding dong.

81
00:02:34,653 --> 00:02:35,453
Delivered.

82
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:37,353
Now, where did your three hundred rupees

83
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:38,113
go?

84
00:02:38,133 --> 00:02:39,773
The restaurant pays the app a commission,

85
00:02:40,213 --> 00:02:41,933
often fifteen to thirty percent.

86
00:02:41,953 --> 00:02:44,673
You pay a platform fee, around fifteen to

87
00:02:44,693 --> 00:02:47,073
eighteen rupees, plus delivery charges and

88
00:02:47,093 --> 00:02:47,893
taxes.

89
00:02:48,213 --> 00:02:49,013
The rider?

90
00:02:49,653 --> 00:02:51,553
Reports say roughly fifty to seventy rupees

91
00:02:51,573 --> 00:02:53,633
an order, and critics say often less.

92
00:02:54,433 --> 00:02:54,773
Tips?

93
00:02:54,793 --> 00:02:56,733
Only three to five percent of orders get

94
00:02:56,753 --> 00:02:57,453
one.

95
00:02:57,473 --> 00:02:58,533
So, maybe tip.

96
00:02:59,033 --> 00:02:59,913
That's the real trick.

97
00:02:59,933 --> 00:03:02,813
Not one magic algorithm, but lots of small

98
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:05,093
predictions, all trying to be right at the

99
00:03:05,113 --> 00:03:05,913
same moment.

100
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:08,653
And as you open the bag, your phone buzzes

101
00:03:08,673 --> 00:03:09,493
one last time.

102
00:03:10,013 --> 00:03:11,393
Your rider is two minutes away.
